# Procurement Opportunity Registry

A Cloudflare-hosted scanner and read-only registry for procurement Bidding Events.

- [Development plan](DEVELOPMENT_PLAN.md)
- [Domain language](CONTEXT.md)
- [Addressability scoring](CONTEXT.md#addressability-scoring-contract)
- [Source integration guide](SOURCE_INTEGRATION_GUIDE.md)
- [Desired Source catalog](source-catalog.yaml)
- [Technical-area taxonomy](tech-area-classification.yaml)

## Local foundation preview

Requirements: Node.js 22 or later.

```bash
npm install
npm run db:migrate:local
npm run db:seed:local
npm run dev
```

The local seed contains clearly marked fixture data. Cloudflare Access is bypassed only for loopback requests and authenticated Amp development portals; other shared and production hostnames fail closed without `TEAM_DOMAIN` and `POLICY_AUD`. The Amp portal exception is removed from production builds. The deployed Worker is protected by a Worker-level Access application with the same 12-hour login policy as DAI CV Formatter: `@dai.com` users and the configured owner account can authenticate through the account's Cloudflare or email one-time PIN login methods.

## Addressability Assessment

Every Source and Client uses the shared scoring contract documented in [CONTEXT.md](CONTEXT.md#addressability-scoring-contract). Minimum value floors are hard gates rather than score inputs: a known positive value below the applicable floor is Excluded, while zero, null, and missing values are treated as unknown and retained for fit scoring. Every retained event starts from an inclusive baseline of 2; DAI-Fit language adds 2 points, Miss-Fit language subtracts 2 points, and the Addressable threshold is 2. The default is therefore Marked unless an event has unopposed Miss-Fit evidence. Structured hard exclusions remain Source-specific.

## Source Inventory

| Source | Status | Access | Current scope |
|---|---|---|---|
| SAM.gov | Deployed; scans are currently quota-constrained | API key | Pre-award notices from MCC, DFC, USTDA, and Department of State |
| Grants.gov | Deployed | Public API | Forecasted and posted opportunities from the approved U.S. organizations |
| TED | Deployed | Public API | Active external-aid planning and competition notices from 12 approved EU Clients |
| EU Funding & Tenders Portal | Deployed | Public API | Forthcoming and open tender calls from the same 12 approved EU Clients |
| dgMarket | Paused; adapter deployed | Public HTML | MCA and EU member-state government consultancy notices |

The complete desired Source scope, Client filters, value thresholds, access notes, and implementation IDs are maintained in the [Source catalog](source-catalog.yaml). The implementation sequence is maintained in the [Source Rollout](DEVELOPMENT_PLAN.md#source-rollout). The backlog currently includes:

- **Public sources:** European Investment Bank procurement and FMO Open Tenders.
- **Login or unattended-authentication sources:** Atamis Supplier Portal, BEIS/Jaggaer, EBRD ECEPP, and GSA eBuy.
- **Interactive two-factor sources:** FCDO eSourcing Portal and SIMAP.

Backlog order is not committed. Each Source still needs API/feed discovery, access and automation review, field-coverage validation, approved Client scope, and a Source-specific Minimum Value Floor before implementation.

## SAM.gov Source

The first Source adapter uses the official SAM.gov Get Opportunities Public API v2. Migration `0002_sam_gov_source.sql` enables it, and the Workflow reads its API key from the `SAM_API_KEY` secret. For local Workflow runs, copy `.dev.vars.example` to `.dev.vars` and supply the key there; never commit that file.

The adapter requests up to 1,000 records per page, follows SAM.gov's zero-based page-index pagination, and scans from the saved cursor with at most two preceding UTC dates of catch-up. Every request is restricted by `organizationCode` to the organizations in `config/sam-gov.yaml`: Millennium Challenge Corporation (`524`), U.S. International Development Finance Corporation (`077`), U.S. Trade and Development Agency (`011`), and Department of State (`019`). Because SAM.gov documents this filter as a single string, the adapter runs one paginated request sequence per organization and deduplicates the combined result. This costs at least four API requests per scan; additional pages consume additional requests.

The adapter collects pre-solicitations, Sources Sought, Special Notices, solicitations, combined synopsis/solicitations, and intent-to-bundle notices. Award, justification, and surplus-sale notices are not pursuit candidates and are omitted.

SAM.gov returns each description as a separate authenticated URL rather than inline search data. The adapter preserves a credential-free description URL in structured Source data but does not spend one API request per notice to download descriptions. Opportunity titles and the other search metadata are therefore available for the initial deterministic classification pass; bulk description ingestion or selective enrichment remains a follow-up decision after measuring the account's request quota.

Addressability configuration version 9 applies the shared scoring contract with Source-specific Minimum Value Floors. For SAM.gov, DOS, MCC, and DFC use $500,000; USTDA and notices owned by a country Millennium Challenge Account use $250,000. Known positive values below the applicable floor, numeric product PSCs, and manufacturing NAICS sectors are hard exclusions. The public pre-award search response does not expose an estimated opportunity value—only award amounts on Award Notices, which are not pursuit candidates—so most live notices are retained with unknown value and scored on DAI-Fit/Miss-Fit evidence. Value extraction remains necessary to exclude notices known to be below the applicable floor.

## Grants.gov Source

The Grants.gov adapter uses the official unauthenticated `search2` JSON API. Grants.gov does not use SAM.gov's three-digit Federal Hierarchy identifiers, so `config/grants-gov.yaml` keeps those four approved identifiers as the shared scope and crosswalks them to Grants.gov's own agency hierarchy. The build fails if the Grants.gov federal codes drift from `config/sam-gov.yaml`.

At the start of each scan, the adapter reads Grants.gov's current agency facet and expands approved parent agencies to their advertised subagencies. It then retrieves only forecasted and posted opportunities for those exact agency codes, up to 1,000 records per page, and rejects any out-of-scope record returned by the API. The current Grants.gov facet advertises Millennium Challenge Corporation as `MCC` and Department of State as `DOS` with its subagencies. It does not currently advertise Development Finance Corporation or Trade and Development Agency, so their approved federal codes remain configured without inventing unsupported Grants.gov codes.

The adapter scans the complete active/forecasted snapshot because `search2` does not document an update-time cursor that would safely detect changes to older opportunities. Repeated snapshots are deduplicated by the shared pipeline. A change between known deadline or amount values becomes a Modification, while newly available descriptions, eligibility, or values enrich the existing Tender in place. Search results provide title, agency, status, dates, and Assistance Listing numbers but not the full synopsis, so the adapter enriches each approved result through the public, unauthenticated `fetchOpportunity` endpoint in bounded batches. It normalizes synopsis or forecast text for Technical Area Classification and stores eligibility, estimated total funding, award ceiling, and award floor. The displayed normalized value uses estimated total funding when present and otherwise the award ceiling, always retaining the exact value basis in structured Source data.

Grants.gov uses the shared scoring contract with Source- and Client-specific Minimum Value Floors: $2 million for DOS, $500,000 for MCC and DFC, and $250,000 for USTDA and country Millennium Challenge Accounts. Known positive values below the applicable floor are excluded; zero, null, and missing values remain eligible for fit scoring.

## TED Source

The TED adapter uses the official unauthenticated `POST https://api.ted.europa.eu/v3/notices/search` Search API. Migration `0004_ted_source.sql` enables it. Its versioned configuration in `config/ted.yaml` combines the supplied `funding IN (external-aid-program)` criterion with a buyer-name filter for DG AGRI, DG CLIMA, DG ECHO, DG CINEA, DG GROW, DG IDEA, DG REA, DG INTPA, DG DEV, DG ENEST, DG MENA, and DG TRADE. The search uses the `ACTIVE` scope, `onlyLatestVersions: false`, and publication-number descending order.

Each scan reads a consistent full snapshot using TED's `ITERATION` mode and opaque continuation tokens, with up to 250 notices per request. The projection stays below TED's 10,000-fields-per-page limit. Planning and competition forms are pursuit candidates; result forms describe post-award outcomes and are not retained. The adapter independently verifies the external-aid funding classification and requires a configured Directorate-General code as a complete token in at least one localized buyer name. Delegated or local buyers without one of those DG codes are not retained.

TED notice identifier plus notice version is the stable Bidding Event identifier. Competition notices use TED's EU Procedure Identifier to link related publications. Planning notices, which do not have an EU Procedure Identifier, use the root notice identifier from any explicit change chain. A `change-notice-version-identifier` maps the consolidated publication to an explicit Modification. Publication dates and the earliest tender or participation deadline are normalized to UTC, and the selected deadline basis remains in structured Source data.

The adapter prefers English procedure titles and descriptions when TED provides them, falls back to another published language without inventing a translation, and retains buyer, place, CPV, funding, change, and notice metadata for provenance. It normalizes only the authoritative procedure-level estimated value; lot-level values remain structured Source data because the notice-based Search API does not expose reliable lot relationships.

TED uses the shared DAI-Fit/Miss-Fit scoring and a €1 million Minimum Value Floor. A known positive EUR procedure estimate below €1 million is Excluded. Missing values remain eligible for fit scoring. Non-EUR values are not compared to the EUR floor because the Source does not provide an authoritative converted value.

## EU Funding & Tenders Portal Source

The EU Funding & Tenders adapter uses the European Commission's official anonymous `POST https://api.tech.ec.europa.eu/search-api/prod/rest/search` Search API and its public `SEDIA` API key. Migration `0005_eu_funding_tenders_source.sql` enables it. Each scan retrieves a complete English snapshot of forthcoming and open calls for tenders (type `0`), 100 records per page, then requires a configured Client code as a complete token in the structured lead contracting authority name. `config/eu-funding-tenders.yaml` is validated against the same 12-Client scope as TED so the two configurations cannot drift.

The Source intentionally does not ingest Funding & Tenders grant topics. Grant-topic search records do not expose an authoritative responsible Directorate-General or lead contracting authority, so applying the requested Client filter would require an unsupported programme-to-DG inference. Calls for tenders expose the contracting authority and an estimated total procedure value directly.

The portal reference is the stable Bidding Event identifier and its notice suffix is removed to link PIN, contract-notice, and ex-ante publications for the same Opportunity. When the portal exposes formal corrigenda, the latest corrigendum notice ID and publication date identify a Modification while the parent portal reference remains in structured Source data. The adapter normalizes publication and submission dates, titles, descriptions, Client, CPV and procedure codes, and the authoritative procedure estimate. It uses the same shared DAI-Fit/Miss-Fit scoring and €1 million rule as TED: a known positive EUR value below €1 million is Excluded, while missing, zero, and non-EUR values remain eligible for fit scoring.

Some Commission tender calls are also published through TED. Registry identity remains Source-specific, so an Opportunity found through both Sources is retained once per Source; cross-Source reconciliation is not part of this adapter.

## dgMarket Source

The dgMarket adapter uses direct HTTP requests against the server-rendered public search at `www2.dgmarket.com`; it does not use Browser Rendering or retrieve subscription-gated notice bodies. Migration `0006_dg_market_source.sql` enabled the Source following confirmation of permission for this systematic access. Migration `0007_disable_dg_market_source.sql` pauses it while its result-set noise is reviewed. Disabled Sources are omitted from registry results and facets, but their records and saved cursor are retained so the Source can be restored without another initial snapshot. `config/dg-market.yaml` defines the two approved scopes. MCA notices use funding agency `1385098` (Millennium Challenge Corporation). EU notices run one search per member state because dgMarket does not reliably accept all 27 notice-contact countries in one request; each search requires the `GOVERNMENT` buyer type. Both scopes require dgMarket's Consultancy category. Public and private organizations, NGOs, contract awards, auctions, and project records are not retained through this adapter.

The search stores its criteria and pagination in a `JSESSIONID` session. The adapter verifies that dgMarket echoed every configured criterion, preserves the session cookie across pages, uses the site's default 25-record page size, throttles requests, caps pages per country, and fails rather than silently accepting changed HTML or dropped filters. The first scan covers 90 days. Later scans use the saved scan cursor with a two-day calendar overlap so delayed indexing and date-boundary changes are rediscovered safely; stable dgMarket notice IDs let the shared pipeline deduplicate that overlap.

List pages provide title, buyer, country of performance, create/change date, deadline, notice type, language, and estimated value. They do not expose the subscription-gated notice body, so initial Technical Area and DAI-Fit/Miss-Fit classification uses the title and other public fields. A displayed zero value is retained in structured Source data as unknown rather than normalized as a real Opportunity value. MCA notices have a $500,000 floor for known USD values, while EU member-state government notices have a €1 million floor for known EUR values. Other currencies are not converted.

## Checks

```bash
npm run validate:config
npm run typecheck
npm test
npm run build
```

`wrangler.jsonc` binds the deployed Worker to the production `procurement-opportunities` D1 database. Local migration and seed commands still use Wrangler's isolated `--local` database; fixture data is never applied remotely.
